  "body": {
    "id": "prop:singleton-inference",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Assume \\(d_e(G)<\\infty\\), a valid source-prime chart package \\(\\mathfrak C_{G,e}\\) is supplied, and fix a successful finite output of \\(\\mathrm{thm:conditional\\mbox{-}geometric\\mbox{-}resolution}\\). If \\(\\mathcal R_e(G,\\Sigma)=\\{r_1(\\Sigma)\\}\\) on \\(\\mathcal K_G\\), then with probability tending to one uniformly on \\(\\mathcal K_G\\), \\(\\widehat{\\mathcal R}_{e,m}\\) has exactly one element; that element is consistent and asymptotically normal with the scalar variance given by the corresponding diagonal entry of the covariance matrix in \\(\\mathrm{thm:regular\\mbox{-}finite\\mbox{-}set\\mbox{-}inference}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Chart_Ge",
      "R_e",
      "G",
      "Sigma",
      "r_vec",
      "Kappa_G",
      "Rhat_m",
      "m",
      "d_e",
      "e"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:source-prime-chart",
      "thm:conditional-geometric-resolution",
      "ass:iid-gaussian",
      "thm:regular-finite-set-inference"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Assume that \\(\\mathcal R_e(G,\\Sigma)=\\{r_1(\\Sigma)\\}\\) throughout \\(\\mathcal K_G\\).  On the events \\(\\mathcal H_m\\) supplied by \\(\\mathrm{thm:regular\\mbox{-}finite\\mbox{-}set\\mbox{-}inference}\\), the estimated set is bijectively matched to this singleton.  Hence it has exactly one element, denoted by \\(\\widehat r_m\\), and\n\\[\n \\inf_{\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal K_G}\\Pr_\\Sigma\\!\\left(\n \\widehat{\\mathcal R}_{e,m}=\\{\\widehat r_m\\}\\right)\\longrightarrow1.\n\\tag{47}\n\\]\nDefine \\(\\widehat r_m=0\\) off that event; (47) makes this modification uniformly negligible.  For singleton sets the Hausdorff distance is absolute error, so the rate conclusion of the preceding theorem gives\n\\[\n |\\widehat r_m-r_1(\\Sigma)|=O_{\\mathbb P}(m^{-1/2})\n\\tag{48}\n\\]\nuniformly, and therefore consistency.  Taking the sole coordinate of the joint branch limit gives\n\\[\n \\sqrt m\\{\\widehat r_m-r_1(\\Sigma)\\}\n \\rightsquigarrow N(0,W_{11}(\\Sigma)),\n\\tag{49}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n W(\\Sigma)=Dr(\\Sigma)D\\pi_G(\\Sigma)V_\\Sigma\n D\\pi_G(\\Sigma)^{\\mathsf T}Dr(\\Sigma)^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\tag{50}\n\\]\nThe negligible off-event definition does not affect the limit.  If \\(W_{11}(\\Sigma)=0\\), (49) denotes the degenerate centered normal law; no variance lower bound has been assumed or used.",
    "justification": "The singleton case connects finite-set inference to the familiar direct-effect estimator and Wald limit.",
    "gap": "Regular half-trek asymptotic normality covers rationally identified coefficients, whereas this corollary also covers a singleton real projection of a higher complex-degree coordinate polynomial.",
    "consumer": "When only one real supported branch survives, applied users obtain an ordinary point estimate, standard error, and confidence interval.",
